.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt EVENTFD 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm eventfd ,
.Nm eventfd2
.Nd a descriptor over a counter, for waking a process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/eventfd.h
.Ft int
.Fn eventfd "unsigned int initval" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn eventfd
returns a new descriptor over a 64-bit counter that starts at
.Fa initval .
The C library calls
.Nm eventfd2 ,
number 328 on i386 and 290 on x86-64, with
.Fa flags ;
.Nm eventfd ,
number 323 on i386 and 284 on x86-64, is the same call with no flags.
The flags are
.Dv EFD_CLOEXEC ,
.Dv EFD_NONBLOCK
and
.Dv EFD_SEMAPHORE .
.Pp
A
.Xr write 2
of eight bytes adds their value to the counter; a write that would
carry the counter past 0xfffffffffffffffe waits, or fails with
.Er EAGAIN
without waiting.
A
.Xr read 2
of eight bytes takes the counter and sets it to zero, or with
.Dv EFD_SEMAPHORE
returns 1 and takes 1; a read of a zero counter waits, or fails with
.Er EAGAIN .
The value is taken before it is copied to the caller, as in Linux, so two
readers never return the same value, and a read whose buffer cannot be
written fails with
.Er EFAULT
and loses what it took.
.Xr poll 2
and
.Xr epoll 2
report the descriptor readable while the counter is not zero and
writable while 1 more fits.
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /fd
names it
.Dq anon_inode:[eventfd] .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn eventfd
returns the descriptor, or -1 with
.Va errno
set.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EMFILE
.It Er EINVAL
.Fa flags
has another bit; or a read or write was shorter than eight bytes, a read
of zero bytes among them, or a
write gave 0xffffffffffffffff.
.It Er EMFILE
The process has no free descriptor below its limit.
.It Er ENOMEM
The kernel had no memory for the counter, a larger descriptor table or
an open file description.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr epoll 2 ,
.Xr poll 2
